        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Fascism</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">1915 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Thinkers</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a>, founder of the Italian Fasces of Combat and dictator of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-gentile">Giovanni Gentile</a>, whom Mussolini called “the philosopher of Fascism”; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a>, leader of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazism">Nazism</a>, a radical German variant; forerunners Georges Sorel, Charles Maurras, Enrico Corradini and Filippo Tommaso Marinetti; José Antonio Primo de Rivera of the Spanish Falange</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Key texts</span><span class="info-stat-val">Filippo Tommaso Marinetti, Manifesto of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/futurism">Futurism</a> (1908); Georges Sorel, Reflections on Violence (1908); <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alceste-de-ambris">Alceste De Ambris</a> and Marinetti, The Manifesto of the Italian Fasces of Combat (1919); Gabriele d’Annunzio and De Ambris, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charter-of-carnaro">Charter of Carnaro</a> (1920); the Charter of Labour (1927); <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-gentile">Giovanni Gentile</a>, The Doctrine of Fascism (1932)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Core ideas</span><span class="info-stat-val">national rebirth (palingenesis) from decadence; ultranationalism, with the nation or race above the individual; a dictatorial leader and a totalitarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a>; political violence and war as means of national rejuvenation; corporatism and class collaboration in place of class struggle; a state-directed economy aiming at autarky; hostility to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a> and democracy</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Today</span><span class="info-stat-val">largely disgraced since 1945, and few parties openly call themselves fascist; neo-fascist and post-fascist are applied to parties rooted in it, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>’s Italian Social Movement, which became the National Alliance; neo-Nazi parties and groups such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>’s NPD and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>’s Golden Dawn; an international surge in popularity since the Great Recession of 2008</span></div>
