    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Fascism arose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>: the term was first used in 1915 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>’s Fasces of Revolutionary Action, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-on-rome">March on Rome</a> brought him to power in 1922, and Hitler’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> took power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1933.</li><li>Fascists exalted the nation or race above all, sought a totalitarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> and saw violence and war as the means of national rebirth; scholars have long disagreed on a precise definition.</li><li>The genocidal and imperialist ambitions of the fascist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis powers</a> resulted in the murder of millions of people in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, above all in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>, and since 1945 fascism has been almost universally condemned.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The fasces, the Roman bundle of rods tied round an axe that gave fascism its name, was used before 1914 mostly by left-wing, liberal and republican movements; it appears on the Sheldonian Theater at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> and on the Lincoln Memorial in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a></p></aside>
    </section>

