    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1915</span><span class="rf-text">The term “fascism” is first used by members of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>’s Fasces of Revolutionary Action</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1919</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> founds the Italian Fasces of Combat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, and the Fascist Manifesto is published</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1922</span><span class="rf-text">After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-on-rome">March on Rome</a>, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-iii">Victor Emmanuel III</a> appoints <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> Prime Minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1925</span><span class="rf-text">After the murder of the Socialist deputy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giacomo-matteotti">Giacomo Matteotti</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> proclaims himself dictator of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1933</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> takes power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and liberal democracy there is dissolved</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1936</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a> begins, with Franco’s army allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> and Hitler against the Spanish Republic</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1939</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> invades <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, beginning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis powers</a> carry out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1945</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> is executed by Italian partisans, Hitler commits suicide and the Nazi regime is dismantled</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/futurism"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/futurism.jpg" alt="Futurism" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/futurism">Futurism</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dynamism_of_a_Biker_(1913)_by_Umberto_Boccioni.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Umberto Boccioni, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The historian Zeev Sternhell traced the roots of fascism to the 1880s and the fin de siècle revolt against materialism, rationalism, positivism, bourgeois society and democracy, an outlook fed by social Darwinism, Arthur de Gobineau’s racialism, Gustave Le Bon’s psychology and the philosophies of Nietzsche and Bergson. Charles Maurras’s integral nationalism and Georges Sorel’s case for political violence fused in the thought of the Italian nationalist Enrico Corradini, who called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> a “proletarian nation” that needed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a> to challenge the “plutocratic” French and British. Filippo Tommaso Marinetti’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/futurism">Futurism</a> added a cult of action and war.</p><p>The first fascist movements emerged in Italy during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>. Revolutionary syndicalists who wanted war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> formed interventionist fasci, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a>, expelled from the Socialist newspaper Avanti!, joined them; the term “fascism” was first used in 1915 by his Fasces of Revolutionary Action. Fascists saw the war as a revolution that had erased the line between civilian and combatant and created a state able to mobilize millions. In 1919 Mussolini founded the Italian Fasces of Combat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. Fear of revolution during the Biennio Rosso of 1919–1920 drew landowners and big business to the fascists, and the movement grew from about a thousand members to some 250,000 by 1921.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="ideas">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Core Ideas</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini.jpg" alt="Benito Mussolini" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mussolini_mezzobusto.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ultranationalism joined to a myth of national rebirth lies at the core of fascism; Roger Griffin calls it a “palingenetic form of populist ultranationalism”. Fascists see the nation as a single organic entity bound by ancestry, to be purged of “decadence” and exalted above all through cults of unity, strength and purity. In The Doctrine of Fascism (1932), partly ghostwritten by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-gentile">Giovanni Gentile</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> wrote that the fascist state was all-embracing: “outside of it no human or spiritual values can exist”. Fascism opposes liberal democracy and multi-party systems and typically supports a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a>, with education and the media turned to indoctrination.</p><p>Fascists presented their economics as a third way between international socialism and free markets: a state-directed mix of private and public ownership, class conflict replaced by class collaboration under the state, and autarky as the goal. Holding social Darwinist views, they opposed welfare as preserving “the degenerate and the feeble” and treated violence as a necessity of an “endless struggle”, which is why most fascist parties built militias such as the Blackshirts and the Brownshirts. Mussolini saw women chiefly as child bearers and men as warriors. Fascism also centres on the demonization of “Others”; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazism">Nazism</a> this meant racial purity and belief in a master race.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread &amp; Variants</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/march-on-rome"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/march-on-rome.jpg" alt="March on Rome" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/march-on-rome">March on Rome</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naples_Fascist_rally_on_24_October_1922_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-on-rome">March on Rome</a> in 1922 brought fascism international attention. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> began to model himself and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> within a month, and in November 1923 the Nazis attempted a “March on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>” that ended in the failed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beer-hall-putsch">Beer Hall Putsch</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a> brought a surge of unrest, which fascist propaganda blamed on “Judeo-Masonic-bolshevik” conspiracies, left-wing internationalism and immigrants. Hitler took power in 1933, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> reached a qualitatively greater fascization than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p>Other regimes were alliances of fascist movements and conservative elites. Francoist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> arose from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a> of 1936–1939 as a compromise between Falangism and clerical authoritarianism, and whether Franco’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> was fascist is debated. After a rising in February 1934 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> had a regime called “Austrofascist”. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> the Iron Guard came to power with General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ion-antonescu">Ion Antonescu</a> in 1940, the only fascist movement outside Germany and Italy to do so without foreign help, and was repressed the next year. In Robert Paxton’s account, Salazar crushed Portuguese fascism after copying some of its techniques. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> was the continent where fascism was most prominent outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. After 1945 neo-fascist movements followed, among them Italy’s Italian Social Movement and the neo-Nazi groups banned in Germany.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="power">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">In Power</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/giacomo-matteotti"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giacomo-matteotti.jpg" alt="Giacomo Matteotti" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/giacomo-matteotti">Giacomo Matteotti</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giacomo_Matteotti_2_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Acerbo Law and an election won through considerable violence and intimidation entrenched the Fascists in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. After the Socialist deputy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giacomo-matteotti">Giacomo Matteotti</a> was kidnapped and murdered by a Fascist, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> declared himself responsible and in January 1925 proclaimed himself dictator; opposition deputies were barred from parliament and censorship introduced. A corporatist system banned strikes and lock-outs, and by 1934 the state holding company IRI held 48.5 percent of Italy’s share capital. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascist-italy">Fascist Italy</a> waged a campaign of mass killings, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a> and the forced starvation of thousands, and expelled 100,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> Cyrenaicans, half the population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a>. It invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> in 1935.</p><p>The Nazis dissolved liberal democracy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1933, enacted racial laws that persecuted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and other groups, interned open homosexuals in concentration camps and mobilized the country for wars of expansion. Germany annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1938, dismantled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> and invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1939, beginning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, which Italy joined in 1940. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> Powers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> led by Nazi Germany took part in the extermination of millions of Jews, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Roma</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinti">Sinti</a> and others in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>. Mussolini was deposed in 1943 and executed by partisans in April 1945, Hitler killed himself two days later, and Nazi leaders were convicted at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> of war crimes and crimes against humanity.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="critiques">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Critiques &amp; Debates</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Fascist parties were contested throughout the interwar years by anti-fascist movements of the centre and left, and during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> resistance arose in every occupied country, from across the ideological spectrum. The most common criticism is that fascism is a tyranny, deliberately and entirely anti-democratic. Federico Finchelstein wrote that it encompassed totalitarianism, state terrorism, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a>, racism and, in the German case, “the most radical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> of the last century: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>”. Critics have called much of Italian fascism a by-product of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>’s unprincipled opportunism, and its shifting positions on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazism">Nazism</a> and race ideologically dishonest: in 1938, on adopting antisemitic laws, Mussolini claimed that Italian fascism had always been antisemitic, though Jewish Italians had been senior fascist officials.</p><p>Scholars disagree on a definition; Ian Kershaw likened the attempt to nailing jelly to the wall. Stanley G. Payne, Roger Griffin and Roger Eatwell, the “new consensus”, centre it on a myth of national rebirth, but critics find this too static and too broad. Robert Paxton defines it as behaviour obsessed with community decline that pursues “internal cleansing and external expansion”; John Lukacs denies that a generic fascism exists. In 1944 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-orwell">George Orwell</a> observed that as used, the word had become almost entirely meaningless.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis powers</a> and the revelation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a> led to an almost universal condemnation of fascism, past and present, and ended it in general as a state ideology. Since 1945 few parties have openly called themselves fascist, and the word is used across the political spectrum as a pejorative, at times even against communist states. Franco’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> outlasted the war, repressing anti-fascist ideologies and democratic institutions, until his death in 1975 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>’s transformation into a liberal democracy.</p><p>In the account of Jean-Yves Camus and Nicolas Lebourg, neo-fascist ideology emerged in 1942. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> the Italian Social Movement became the self-described “post-fascist” National Alliance; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> neo-Nazi movements have been formed and banned under constitutional law that forbids <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazism">Nazism</a>. Since the Great Recession of 2008 fascism has seen an international surge in popularity. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> the Golden Dawn, widely considered neo-Nazi, won seats in parliament, and in 2020 its leader Nikolaos Michaloliakos and six others were found guilty of running a criminal organization. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> the 2017 Unite the Right rally drew neo-fascists and neo-Nazis, and groups such as the Proud Boys and Patriot Front were founded. Whether Putin’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> is fascist is debated: Timothy Snyder says it is, while Payne, Griffin and Marlène Laruelle disagree.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Fascism is a far-right, ultranationalist ideology that arose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a> and came to power under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> in 1922 and Hitler in 1933. Its regimes destroyed democracy, persecuted minorities and opponents and waged wars of conquest, and the genocidal ambitions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis powers</a> murdered millions, above all in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>. Largely disgraced since 1945, it survives in neo-fascist movements.</em></p>

